# Plan

This is the board — the place where the team's work on the Sales workbook lives
as data. It is also the proof of a claim that sounds wrong the first time you
hear it: **declarative is not immutable**. A workbook is HTML you author by hand,
but it is also a running store. The same document you read is the one the agents
write back into. Tasks move from `:todo` to `:done` *in this file*. The document
is the database.

This page teaches three things by doing them: task value declarations, agents
**claiming** work without trampling each other, and a full **harness** — an
agent, a flow, the tasks it operates on, and a test that gates the whole thing —
all in one file. Everything here references the units built upstream:
[[Score]], [[Enrich]], [[Pipeline]], and the [[Analyst]] agent.

## Tasks are values, not markup

A task is a value of the canonical `Task` struct. It is a record — a `defstruct`
whose field *types are inferred from the default values*. There is no DSL, no
schema language, no typespec leading the way. The shape is the struct; the types
fall out of `""`, `:todo`, `nil`.

server compute :board do
  defmodule Task do
    defstruct title: "", status: :todo, owner: nil, ref: nil
  end

  # The status of a task is one of these atoms — an enum is just a set.
  @statuses [:todo, :doing, :done]

  # `owner` resolves against the workspace symbol table to a real person, and
  # `ref` is a work:// deep link to the unit the task touches. Both are checked
  # at the weave gate: an owner who isn't a defined person, or a ref that points
  # nowhere, fails the build before any bundle ships.
  def statuses, do: @statuses

  def valid?(%Task{status: s}) when s in @statuses, do: true
  def valid?(%Task{}), do: false
end

The `:board` unit is **server** compute — native Elixir on the BEAM, not wasm.
It runs in the nexus because the board is shared, multi-tenant state: every
agent and every teammate reads and writes the same list. That isolation is the
nexus's job, not a sandbox's.

## The board, as data

Here is the actual work. `data` declares typed values of a struct; it renders by
default, so this list *is* the board you see on the page. No SQL, no JSON —
typed values of `Task`.

server data :tasks, of: Task, from: values([
  %Task{title: "Wire enrich → CRM API",       status: :done,  owner: "ana", ref: :enrich},
  %Task{title: "Tune scoring weights",         status: :doing, owner: "ana", ref: :score},
  %Task{title: "Forecast next-quarter pipe",   status: :todo,  owner: nil,   ref: :forecast},
  %Task{title: "Draft outreach for hot leads", status: :todo,  owner: nil,   ref: :analyst},
  %Task{title: "Risk-flag thin-margin leads",  status: :todo,  owner: nil,   ref: :risk}
])

Two of these tasks have no `owner` yet (`nil`). They are **unclaimed**. The
backlinks in `ref` point at real units elsewhere in this workbook —
[[Forecast]], [[Analyst]], [[Risk]] — so `work why` can trace why each task
exists and `work near` can show what it touches.

## Claiming work: drafts, locks, and fences

If the document is the store and many agents write to it, you need the same
discipline a database gives you: don't let two writers stomp the same row. The
work format does this with **drafts** and **scopes** — no global mutex, no
"agent, please wait."

A **draft** is a scoped, uncommitted overlay on the document. An agent opens a
draft, mutates tasks *inside* the draft, and the draft commits atomically or not
at all. A **scope** narrows what the draft may touch — here, exactly one task —
so two agents claiming two different tasks never contend. The narrowed scope is
the **fence**: a write that strays outside it is rejected at commit.

server compute :claims do
  # `claim/2` is a compare-and-swap over the document. It opens a draft fenced
  # to a single task, and only commits if that task is still unclaimed. If
  # another agent won the race, the draft is discarded and we report it — no
  # partial write, no lost update.
  def claim(title, who) do
    draft scope: {:task, title} do
      case fetch(title) do
        {:ok, %Task{owner: nil} = t} ->
          commit(%Task{t | owner: who, status: :doing})
          {:ok, :claimed}

        {:ok, %Task{owner: other}} ->
          {:error, {:already_claimed, other}}

        :error ->
          {:error, :no_such_task}
      end
    end
  end

  # Finishing is the same shape: a fenced draft flips status to :done, but only
  # the owner who claimed it may close it. The fence keeps one agent from
  # closing another's work.
  def finish(title, who) do
    draft scope: {:task, title} do
      case fetch(title) do
        {:ok, %Task{owner: ^who} = t} ->
          commit(%Task{t | status: :done})
          {:ok, :done}

        {:ok, %Task{owner: other}} ->
          {:error, {:not_owner, other}}

        :error ->
          {:error, :no_such_task}
      end
    end
  end
end

Because the draft commits over `:tasks` — the live `data` declaration above —
the rendered board updates the moment a claim lands. The page is not a snapshot
of the store; the page **is** the store. That is what "declarative is not
immutable" means in practice.

## A full harness in one file

Now the real point of this page: a workbook unit is not just a function or a
list — it can be a complete, self-contained working system. Below is one
harness: an **agent** that claims and works tasks, a **flow** that drives it
over the backlog, the **tasks** it operates on (declared above), and a **test**
that gates the whole thing. The weave gate runs that test; a red test ships no
bundle.

### The agent

The dispatcher is a brain — a system prompt plus a model plus tools — and its
loop runs in server Elixir, backed by the nexus. Its memory is typed data: the
board itself. It hands real outreach drafting to the [[Analyst]] agent and only
manages the queue.

agent :dispatcher do
  model "minimax/minimax-m3"

  system """
  You manage the sales backlog. Pick the highest-leverage unclaimed task,
  claim it for the agent best suited to it, do the work via your tools, and
  close it. Never touch a task you do not own. Prefer scored, high-revenue
  leads. If no task is ready, stop — do not invent work.
  """

  tools [
    Claims.claim/1,
    Claims.finish/1,
    Analyst.draft_outreach/1
  ]

  # The agent's decision step: given the open backlog, choose one task and the
  # specialist that should own it. Routing is by the unit the task references.
  def route(%Task{ref: :analyst}), do: :analyst
  def route(%Task{ref: :risk}),    do: :risk
  def route(%Task{ref: _}),        do: :dispatcher
end

### The flow

The flow is orchestration — sequential `step`s, with a `parallel` fan-out where
the work is independent. It pulls the open tasks, claims each one under its
fence, runs the matching specialist, and closes it. Two claims in the same
`parallel` block touch different fenced scopes, so they never contend.

server flow :work_board do
  step :pull, do: query(:tasks, where: &(&1.status == :todo))

  step :assign, after: :pull do
    parallel for task <- result(:pull) do
      who = dispatcher().route(task)

      with {:ok, :claimed} <- claims().claim(task.title, who),
           {:ok, _output}  <- run(who, task) do
        claims().finish(task.title, who)
      else
        {:error, {:already_claimed, _}} -> :skip
        {:error, reason} -> {:failed, task.title, reason}
      end
    end
  end

  step :report, after: :assign do
    done = query(:tasks, where: &(&1.status == :done))
    %{closed: length(done), open: length(result(:pull)) - length(done)}
  end
end

### The test — the gate

A workbook is not done because the code parses; it is done because the test
passes. The weave gate runs this test as part of `work bundle`. If `assert`
fails, the build is red and **no `salesapp.html` ships**. The test exercises the
claim/fence invariants directly — the contract that makes concurrent board
writes safe.

server test :board_invariants do
  test "claiming an open task assigns the owner and moves it to :doing" do
    {:ok, :claimed} = claims().claim("Forecast next-quarter pipe", "ana")
    {:ok, t} = claims().fetch("Forecast next-quarter pipe")
    assert t.owner == "ana"
    assert t.status == :doing
  end

  test "a second claim on the same task is fenced out, not lost" do
    {:ok, :claimed} = claims().claim("Risk-flag thin-margin leads", "ana")
    assert {:error, {:already_claimed, "ana"}} =
             claims().claim("Risk-flag thin-margin leads", "bo")
  end

  test "only the owner may close a task" do
    {:ok, :claimed} = claims().claim("Draft outreach for hot leads", "ana")
    assert {:error, {:not_owner, "ana"}} =
             claims().finish("Draft outreach for hot leads", "bo")
  end

  test "every declared task is structurally valid at the gate" do
    for t <- query(:tasks) do
      assert board().valid?(t)
    end
  end
end

## What this page is

The board you scrolled through is live state: a typed `data` declaration the
[[Pipeline]] feeds, that the `:dispatcher` agent edits through fenced drafts,
and that the weave gate guards with `:board_invariants` before anything ships.
Read top to bottom it is a working backlog; read as code it is a CAS-safe
mutation system over the document itself. Same file, both true — because the
document is the store.

Next, the work leaves the building: see [[Deploy]] for how `work bundle` weaves
this whole tree into one `salesapp.html`.
